    board: file CF-158 — the CF-119 lock adjudicator calls an open fd a held lock
    
    Review lane tick code-factory-review-2026-11-04-03-41-13. Reviewed commit
    a012d02180c1ea53e979b65b545d9533f7d2da07 ("fix(board-lock): adjudicate board/.lock as
    residue and ship the holder-proof classifier", closes CF-119), reviewed at tip 7989ba3
    and re-probed at the filing tip after a sibling landed a CF-156 closeout.
    
    scripts/board_lock.py's load-bearing claim is that HELD requires a proven live holder.
    Both of its proofs over-accept:
    
    - An OPEN FD is not a lock. A process that only opens the path — no flock at all —
      makes the classifier print `HELD holder=pid-...`. Any reader (tail -f, an editor,
      another tool) therefore tells every lane to WAIT: that is the phantom ownership
      barrier CF-119 was filed to remove, re-entered through the instrument built to
      remove it.
    - A SHARED flock is not ownership either: a LOCK_SH holder prints HELD while two
      shared holders demonstrably coexist (a shared lock excludes nobody).
    
    The gate that ships with it cannot see either. Replacing the fd branch with a constant
    (`holders = []`) leaves `SELFTEST: PASS` with both section-33 rows green, because the
    fixture holds a real flock, which satisfies either proof. The consumer count globs
    `scripts/boardctl*` only, so a flock user elsewhere under `scripts/` still prints
    `consumers=0` — the number the verdict offers as proof that "no shipped script opens it".
    
    Filed CF-158 (pending, P3, complexity 2) with a one-line reproducible probe; the repair
    is future work. Also recorded a corroborating note on CF-142: the selftest auth-stats arm
    red-ran once under load (`SELFTEST: FAIL (1)`) and passed on a re-run minutes later with
    no tree change, so the gate is nondeterministic in both directions.
    
    Id collision disclosed: the row was drafted and first written as CF-157; while this tick
    was committing, a sibling landed 7c51fbe and consumed CF-157 for an unrelated README
    finding. The uncommitted draft was rolled back, main fast-forwarded through the sibling
    landings (7c51fbe, d6d023e — parity 0 0, and neither touched the board), and the row
    re-filed as CF-158.
    
    - `.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl`: +CF-158, CF-142 note
    - `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`: `task_created` event 214
    - `docs/audits/code-factory-review-CF-158.md`: this review's receipt
    
    Gates at the landed tree (7c51fbe + these board rows): `bash tests/selftest.sh` ->
    `SELFTEST: PASS`, rc=0, 103 PASS rows, `132/132 rows parse`, loadavg 5.81; the same gate
    passed twice in a clean clone at d3b06a8 with porcelain 0; `bash scripts/boardctl validate`
    -> `RESULT: OK (3 warnings)`, the 3 being pre-existing (CF-034 free-form fields, headerless
    board — the CF-154 class); `gh run list --repo coding-hermes/code-factory` -> `[]` (no
    workflows). CF-158's probe re-run as extracted from the board row -> rc=0, same three lines.
    
    Guard bypass disclosed: the pre-commit guard refuses a board/docs-only diff with a Tier 1
    DEGRADED PASS (`lint=no staged files`) and leaves HEAD unmoved, so this commit uses
    `git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null commit` — the repository's documented board-only precedent.
    Tier 1 was run explicitly before it: secrets clean, tests (full) PASS, lint skipped because
    no implementation file is staged.
    
