 {"id":"CF-155","title":"P4 \u2014 docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md still states 29 selftest sections while tests/selftest.sh has 30: \u00a730 landed at 1b4b0b6 and the row's own shipped drift probe exits 1 at HEAD","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":1,"created_at":"2026-11-04T03:02:01Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T03:02:01Z","detail":{"evidence":[]},"reasoning":"Measured at code-factory@c29c024 (re-verified at gh/main da84b0c). docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md states 29 numbered selftest sections twice (:7 '29 sections \u2014 plus two lettered sub-arms', :61 '`d02916e`, `tests/selftest.sh` has 29 numbered sections plus two lettered sub-arms (\u00a74b, \u00a77b)') and its reverse-direction table stops at \u00a729 (:69-96 \u2014 no \u00a730 row). tests/selftest.sh has 30: `say \"30. README Status rows 1 and 3 cite the interpreter-qualified --check\"` landed at 1b4b0b6 (2026-11-03 21:35:05, 'Addresses CF-093'), and the later c9848d7 ('docs(sync): reconcile the board census and the ACCEPTANCE-PLAN snapshot at 2c48149') re-anchored the row/event deltas while leaving the section count and the \u00a729-terminated table untouched. The instrument CF-099 shipped for exactly this drift (docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh) therefore exits 1 at HEAD with 'DRIFT: plan says 29 sections, tests/selftest.sh has 30'. Nothing forces a new numbered section to update the plan: no selftest arm reads docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md, so the gate stays green (SELFTEST: PASS at c29c024) while the plan describes a tree that no longer exists. DISTINCT FROM CF-113 (the census regex cannot see LETTERED sub-arms, \u00a77b) and CF-121 (the probe's snapshot leg is a tautology) and CF-115 (legs the probe certifies without measuring): this is a NUMBERED section the census sees, dropped by the plan side, and the probe's own measured leg is what reds. CF-099 is complete and was correct at its own closure commit a9d0cc8; the rot arrived later with 1b4b0b6, so this is fresh drift, not a reopen. ch:trace row=CF-154 spec=docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md wave=none:qa-tick-no-manifest test=tests/selftest.sh doc=docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md prompt=prompts/docs.md evidence=~/.hermes/stand-in/pm/code-factory-docs/docs-qa-2026-11-03-evidence.md witness=cmd:probe-153 verdict=none:filed-not-judged commit=none:not-yet-fixed memory=done:duckbrain-6a265050","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree from the repo root: the probe below prints `30`, then the two ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md lines that say `29` (`:7` and `:61`), then `DRIFT: plan says 29 sections, tests/selftest.sh has 30`, then `probe_rc=1`.\n1. `bash docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh` exits 0 at the fix commit and its `measured:` line reports `sections=30`.\n2. The section count stated in docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md equals `grep -cE '^say \"[0-9]+\\.' tests/selftest.sh`, and the reverse-direction table carries a row for `\u00a730` naming what governs it (README Status rows 1 and 3, board CF-093) beside the existing \u00a71-\u00a729 rows.\n3. A gate can fail on this drift: adding a numbered `say \"NN. ...\"` section without updating docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md makes `bash tests/selftest.sh` (or the committed probe wired into it) exit non-zero, and removing that section makes it pass again (the red-proof arm is named in the output, never the substring FAIL alone).\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fix commit.","probe":"grep -cE '^say \"[0-9]+\\.' tests/selftest.sh; grep -n 'sections \u2014 plus two lettered' docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md; grep -n 'has 29 numbered sections' docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md; bash docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh; echo \"probe_rc=$?\""}
 {"id":"CF-156","title":"P2 \u2014 an unresolved secret-scanning alert sits open on the PUBLIC repo: a slack-token-shaped fixture is still reachable at commit 8edba9f and nothing in the fleet triages alerts","status":"complete","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T03:25:52Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T03:45:11Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"17632956f9c494c25d2914e6cceaaa9b1323498a2aaf524d77901ff58d6d4cdb","evidence":[{"run_id":"783e56f0","ts":"2026-11-04T03:45:11Z","tree_fp":"fee51a3742f0d2b254bef27cb24eb1bb2ae082076f9b9e1d6ac326bc47463429","artifacts":[".gitreins/history/2026-11-04/783e56f0/verdict.json"]}]},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 on a fresh clone of origin/main (dae4e7a) with gh: `repos/coding-hermes/code-factory/secret-scanning/alerts?state=open` returns exactly 1 alert and `?state=resolved` returns 0. Alert 1 has been OPEN since 2026-11-02T11:22:58Z \u2014 across at least six releng/readme/qa ticks \u2014 with secret_type `slack_api_token`, provider Slack, `validity: unknown`, and `publicly_leaked: true`, on a repository whose visibility is PUBLIC. Nothing has ever resolved or dismissed an alert in this repo.\n\nThe reported location is `.hermes/scripts/test_secret_scrub.py:30` at commit 8edba9f (\"fix(security): SEC-001 write-time secret scrub for chat-archive ingestion\"), blob 7d38bc75d6071de0ad2f3c4344cc128fd668df0d. That path is ABSENT from the current tree (`git ls-files --error-unmatch` fails), so the alert is historical rather than live-tree: the blob is still reachable from public history (`git cat-file -e 8edba9f` succeeds). The value is a scrubber test fixture \u2014 its neighbours in the same table are documented placeholders (AWS's own public example access-key id, the sha1/sha256 of the empty string, `Sup3rFakePassw0rd`) \u2014 so rotation is probably not the remedy. But \"probably a fixture\" is a judgement someone has to RECORD, not a state the repo can keep ignoring: a scraped Slack token with `validity: unknown` is the class of finding a public repo is expected to close out.\n\nThe posture asymmetry is the actual defect. Secret scanning AND push protection are enabled (`security_and_analysis.secret_scanning.status=enabled`, `secret_scanning_push_protection.status=enabled`), so a new push of that shape is blocked at the door \u2014 yet there is no triage loop behind the alert: 0 resolved, 0 dismissed, no owner, no board row until this one. Push protection also means the repository's own fixture must be documented so a future editor understands why the shape is there and does not \"fix\" it by rotating something fictional.\n\nNot filed as a duplicate: CF-120 records the protection SETTINGS (secret scanning on, branch protection absent) but says nothing about the one open alert; CF-050/CF-086 cover absolute host paths, not credential shapes. This row is the alert's triage and the fixture's documentation, and it is P2 because it is an unresolved leaked-shaped credential in public history rather than a wording defect.","acceptance_criteria":"1. `gh api 'repos/coding-hermes/code-factory/secret-scanning/alerts?state=open' --jq 'length'` prints 0, with EITHER a dismissal carrying a resolution comment that names the fixture file and the commit it came from, OR \u2014 if the owner rules the value real \u2014 a recorded rotation plus the history remedy the owner chose. A silent `state=open` count of 0 achieved by deleting the alert is not acceptance.\n2. The in-tree fixture stops re-tripping the provider detector: the file that carries the slack-shaped fixture (or its successor) documents with a comment that the value is a scrubber fixture and names the GitHub alert it once raised, and a commit touching that file produces no NEW alert (`gh api 'repos/coding-hermes/code-factory/secret-scanning/alerts' --jq 'length'` unchanged).\n3. The row records who triaged it and the reason code used, so the next releng tick can re-measure criterion 1 without re-deciding the question.\n4. The remedy introduces no new absolute host paths, credentials, or internal endpoints: `git grep -nE 'xox[bprs]-' -- . | grep -v <the documented fixture path>` is empty and the tree's ruff/selftest gates still pass (`bash tests/selftest.sh` -> `SELFTEST: PASS`).","probe":null,"commit_hash":"f54c54b5c8bd94e603f6419f90cbbe5b96e27ae6","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"Alert 1 dismissed as used_in_tests with a comment naming .hermes/scripts/test_secret_scrub.py line 30 at commit 8edba9f; docs/SECURITY-TRIAGE.md records the triage and the re-measure commands. Verified: alerts?state=open length 0, alerts/1 resolution used_in_tests, selftest PASS at f54c54b, tier2 verdict 1fd1b009 PASS."}
 {"id":"CF-157","title":"README asserts all fourteen shipped lanes are enabled=true, but the shipped census recipe prints code-factory-qa enabled=false","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T03:45:04Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T03:45:04Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"58af89c79270fa05459395960899c841d97d395d840771af52e29b3de9dd924c"},"reasoning":"README.md line 205 tells a stranger that the reference deployment \"prints nineteen rows: the fourteen above, all `enabled=true`\". The command immediately above it \u2014 the shipped census recipe `docs/audits/cf089-census-recipe.sh`, which tests/selftest.sh \u00a728 keeps in byte parity with the README block \u2014 prints nineteen rows today with `code-factory-qa enabled=false`, exit 0. The scheduler auto-disabled that lane at 2026-11-04T03:27:07Z on a 90.3% failure rate (84/93 ticks), so the sentence is now contradicted by the documented command two paragraphs above it. The row's own hedge (\"a live scheduler can differ in both directions\") makes the fix wording, not fleet state: the enabled figure must be dated and attributed to the census, not asserted in the present tense.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: `bash docs/audits/cf089-census-recipe.sh` prints nineteen rows and one of them is `code-factory-qa enabled=false`, exit 0, while README.md line 205 says all fourteen shipped lanes are `enabled=true`.\n1. README.md no longer asserts the enabled state of the fourteen shipped lanes in the present tense: the sentence names the measurement date and says the census command is what decides the live value.\n2. `grep -c 'all `enabled=true`' README.md` prints 0 on the committed tree.\n3. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS`, exit 0, and still counts \u00a728 (census/mirror byte parity) as PASS \u2014 the census code block is not edited, only the prose that reads it.\n4. `bash docs/audits/cf089-census-recipe.sh` still prints nineteen rows (the fix does not cha